        This is a presentation of geometry for a reader willing to follow a text with formulas, new concepts, diagrams, and computations, but who does not need to follow any specifically analytical computations (so, no derivatives and no integrals).  Various geometries are presented, each with particularly engaging examples of problems that are formulated in that particular geometry.  After a short chapter that addresses the question ``What is geometry?'' by presenting a survey of the chapters to follow, the reader is introduced to what might be the most familiar territory: Euclidean geometry. There o
