        One of the extensions of the classical coloring problem in which it is necessary to find the chromatic number \(\chi\) of the Eucledian plane is to stipulate two forbidden distances \(\{1,d\}\) instead of single one. This article introduce a simple proof for the lower bound of the chromatic number of the Euclidean plane with two forbidden distances, using a graph with only 16 vertices.
