        Summary: The multi-fold chromatic number of the plane \(\chi_m\) is the smallest number of colors \(k\), sufficient to color each point of the Euclidean plane in exactly \(m\) colors, so that for any pair of points at a unit distance from each other, two corresponding \(m\)-subsets of \(k\)-set do not contain any common color. We consider upper bounds for \(m\)-fold chromatic numbers of the plane. Our main result is that for any \(m\) the inequality \(\chi_m<(1+2/\sqrt 3)2^\cdot m+3.501\) holds.
