   1999  Zhu, Xuding  |  Construction of uniquely \(H\)-colorable graphs  |  J. Graph Theory 30, No. 1, 1-6 (1999).  [review]  https://zbmath.org/1309148
        Let \(G\) and \(H\) be graphs. An \(H\)-coloring of \(G\) is a mapping \(f:V(G)\to V(H)\) such that \(f(x)f(y)\in E(H)\) if \(xy\in E(G)\). (A proper \(n\)-coloring is the special case where \(H=K_n\).) A graph \(H\) is a core if \(H\) is not \(H'\)-colorable for all proper subgraphs \(H'\) of \(H\). A graph \(G\) is uniquely \(H\)-colorable if there is a surjective \(H\)-coloring of \(G\), that is unique up to automorphisms of \(H\). It is shown that if the chromatic number \(\chi(G)\) is large enough (depending on \(H)\) then the categorical product \(H\times G\) is uniquely \(H\)-colorable.
