        [For the entire collection see Zbl 0559.00001.]    A graph is uniquely colourable if it admits exactly one partition of V(G) into \(\chi\) (G) colour classes. If G is uniquely colourable and \(\chi (G)=k\), then we say that G is uniquely k-colourable. The main results of the paper concern the following two functions: \(u_ k(n)=\min \{e(G):| G| =n\) and G is uniquely k-colourable\(\}\) and \(r(k)=\min \{| G|:\) G is uniquely k-colourable and \(cl(G)=k-1\}\). We prove that \(u_ k(n)=n(k-1)-\left( \begin{matrix} k\\ 2\end{matrix} \right)\) and we present some results on the extremal graphs. For r
